  abstract     = {LEO satellite networks provide low propagation delay, broad coverage, and rapid deployment, but their changing topology and frequent handovers demand routing that is both adaptive and computationally light. QRL-SR applies quantum reinforcement learning to this problem under the small size, weight, power, and onboard-computing limits of LEO spacecraft. The design addresses three linked requirements: adaptation to a time-varying network, explicit representation of orbital geometry, and a lightweight trainable policy. It converts two-line-element celestial data into orbital coordinates to locate satellites accurately as time advances and uses QRL to reduce the policy's trainable-parameter count. Its routing objective is to lower free-space path loss, end-to-end delay, and the number of inter-satellite-link hops. Experiments show routing performance that is comparable to or better than conventional algorithms while consuming substantially fewer computational resources.},
